{"kind":"assert","text":"C3 ruling on 71ee37a4. Build the alternative: rule 5 reads the latest\nsigning only. `Custody.send` gains no index, and the record gains no\nfield.\n\nReason. A selected earlier signing gives each record two \"current\"\nsignings. Each rule that says \"the latest signing\" (5.2, 5.3, 5.5,\nrule 5 of section 6, and the table of section 7) would then need a\nsecond reading. One more signing costs less than that. The note\nalready says that the client does not have to prove an earlier\nsigning dead before it signs again.\n\nThe C3 head changes section 4 of the client note:\n- Rule 5: \"At each submit the client builds the intent from the\n  draft's values at the replica's head. If the latest signing is\n  live and equals the built intent apart from `notAfter`, the client\n  sends that signing's bytes again.\"\n- Rule 6 keeps its words.\n- One new paragraph after the rules: \"The client does not send an\n  earlier signing again, because `Custody` sends only the latest\n  signing. When the built intent equals an earlier live signing, the\n  client signs it again. Both signings have one meaning. If the scope\n  seals the earlier one, the later one gets `mismatch`, section 5.3\n  finds the receipt of the earlier envelope, and rule 1 of section 6\n  reads `recorded`.\"\n\nYour example then goes: A is signing 0, B is signing 1, and the\nsecond submit of A is signing 2, by `resign` on a `refused` record.\n\nTests in C3:\n1. The example: three signings, and no copy of signing 0 leaves\n   after signing 1 exists.\n2. A late copy of signing 0 is sealed after signing 2 is sent. The\n   status is `recorded`, and custody reads `accepted` for signing 0.\n3. The bound: a member who alternates two values gets `full` with\n   reason `signings` at 8 kept envelopes, the draft keeps the\n   values, and a submit signs again after one signing closes.\n\nThe review request names this change of the note.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}